        "text_dwVE4f": {
          "type": "text",
          "name": "t:names.text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Temple jewellery originated in South India during the rule of the Chola and Pandya dynasties around the 9th century. Originally, these ornaments were created to decorate the idols of Hindu gods and goddesses in temples. Skilled artisans crafted them using gold and precious stones, carving sacred symbols of different Gods and Goddess, different animals such as peacocks, elephants, tigers, and lotus motifs that represent prosperity, strength, purity, and divine protection. Over time, this jewellery became popular among royal families and devotees who wore similar designs during festivals and religious ceremonies. The jewellery is traditionally handmade using techniques such as repoussé and intricate stone setting, giving it a rich antique appearance. Temple jewellery later gained wider popularity through classical Indian dance forms like Bharatanatyam, where dancers wear elaborate ornaments to reflect divine beauty and tradition. Today, temple jewellery is closely associated with bridal fashion and cultural heritage in India.</p>",
            "width": "100%",
            "max_width": "normal",
            "alignment": "left",
