        "text_9HHtfP": {
          "type": "text",
          "name": "t:names.text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Bead jewellery is among the earliest forms of personal adornment in human history. Archaeological evidence suggests that beads were used as ornaments as early as 70,000–100,000 years ago in regions such as Africa and the Middle East. One of the earliest civilizations known for producing bead jewellery was the Indus Valley Civilization around 2500 BCE, where artisans crafted beads from carnelian, agate, lapis lazuli, and terracotta. Bead jewellery is known for its colourful and vibrant appearance, as beads can be made in many shapes, sizes, and colours. In ancient India, queens and royal women known to admire and wear bead necklaces made from precious stones and pearls. Traditionally, artisans shaped and polished beads by hand and threaded them together using silk or cotton thread. In modern times, jewellers produce beads from glass, crystals, gemstones, ceramics, and metals, bamboos, cotton, different types of metals such as gold, silver, zinc, copper, e.t.c, plastics, using both machines and handcrafting techniques to create innovative designs.</p>",
